4. EXTREMELY IMPORTANT — MINIMAL TEXT UX
==================================================

This is one of the highest-priority requirements.

The interface must NOT become filled with unnecessary text.

We want high information value, not high word count.

A new clinician, nurse, front-desk employee, billing executive or administrator should be able to recognise what a screen does within a few seconds.

Follow these rules:

1. Use short, meaningful headings.

Examples:
"Today's Patients"
"Pending Review"
"New Order"
"Active Medications"
"Care Plan"
"Claims"

2. Avoid repetitive explanatory paragraphs.

3. Do not display technical architecture descriptions on ordinary screens.

4. Use progressive disclosure.

Show essential information first.

Reveal additional details through:
- Tabs
- Expandable sections
- Drawers
- Popovers
- Details pages
- Secondary actions

5. Prefer structured information over prose.

Use:
- Tables
- Timelines
- Compact cards
- Status indicators
- Charts when meaningful
- Short labels
- Clear primary buttons

6. Avoid displaying 15–20 metric cards just to fill space.

7. Every dashboard must focus on the current user's immediate decisions and work.

8. Never repeat the same metric in several cards without a clear purpose.

9. Use concise tooltips for uncommon medical and operational terms.

10. Empty states should be short and useful.

Example:
"No pending results"
"New results will appear here."

11. Use one prominent primary action per screen.

12. Keep secondary actions available but visually quiet.

13. Do not show advanced filters permanently unless frequently needed.

14. Avoid long breadcrumbs when a short title and Back action are sufficient.

15. Do not hide clinically important safety information merely to reduce text.

16. Never abbreviate important clinical information ambiguously.

17. Use whitespace thoughtfully, but avoid giant unused gaps.

18. Avoid unnecessarily large cards when compact tables communicate more information.

19. No decorative metrics, invented KPIs or meaningless charts.

20. Use plain, professional American English.

Critical rule:
Reduce unnecessary TEXT, not important FUNCTIONALITY.

Every specified feature must remain discoverable.

The application should achieve a balance between clinical information density and excellent visual clarity.

